{0 Attribute anchors}

Djot attributes ([{#id}]) attach {e explicit identifiers} to inline spans
and blocks. This spec defines the {e attribute id} as an anchor kind
alongside heading identifiers.

This is a {b foundational} spec. It describes the anchor namespace and
its resolution semantics; the navigation features that consume it
(go-to-definition, find-references, diagnostics, completion) reference
this page for the anchor model and add only their feature-specific
behavior.

{1 Motivation}

Without attributes, a fragment ([#...]) could only target a {b heading},
matched by its text or identifier ([[[note#Section One]]]).

Djot attributes let {e any} inline span or block carry an explicit id:

{[
{#intro}
## Overview

The [key term]{#key-term} is defined here.

{#aside}
> A blockquote with an id.
]}

A block attribute is written on {b its own line, above} the block it names —
a heading included. Pandoc's trailing form ([## Overview \{#intro\}]) is not
read: the braces attach to nothing, so no anchor is recorded {e and} the
heading text keeps a trailing space. An attribute line below a block attaches
to nothing. Inline attributes are the exception, and follow their span:
[ [key term]\{#key-term\} ].

This makes the id a first-class, author-controlled anchor. Unlike a
heading's text (which changes when the heading is edited), an attribute id
is stable and can pin an {e arbitrary sub-inline} target.

{1 Anchor collection}

An {b attribute anchor} is any inline or block node whose djot attributes
have an id ({!Djot.Attr.id} returns [Some id]). See
{!Oystermark.Note.Anchor.of_doc}.

{2 Sources}

{ul
  {- {b Block attributes}: the attributes of a block (paragraph,
     blockquote, list, code block, ...).}
  {- {b Inline attributes}: [[span]\{#id\}], or [\{#id\}] after any inline.}
  {- {b Heading ids}: an id written above a heading is on its section, and
     is the heading's identifier. It is a heading anchor, not an attribute
     anchor as well, so one authored id is one anchor — see
     {!page-"feature-diagnostics".duplicate_ids}.}
}

{2 Location precision}

Each anchor records a [Djot.Textloc.t] from the node it annotates:

{ul
  {- A block anchor records the {b [ \{#id\} ] line} it was written on,
     through the block it attributes ({!Oystermark.Note.Anchor.extent}), so
     navigation lands on the line the author wrote the id on rather than on
     the block below it.}
  {- Heading anchors record the heading's {b line}.}
  {- Inline anchors record a {b line and character}, because the
     annotated span has a column-precise location. Navigation to an
     inline anchor may therefore land on a character offset, not only the
     start of a line.}
}

{1 Resolution}

Fragment resolution ([note#id] and self-reference [#id]) matches the
fragment string, in order, against:

{ol
  {- headings, by identifier or by {!Oystermark.Parse.Common.heading_key}
     of their text;}
  {- attribute ids, by exact string comparison.}
}

The first match wins. A [note#id] wikilink or a [(#id)] markdown-link
fragment that matches an attribute id resolves to that anchor's path and
location, see {!Oystermark.Vault.Index.resolve}.

{2 Namespace and precedence}

Heading identifiers and attribute ids share {b one} fragment namespace.
When a fragment string could match more than one kind, the order above is
authoritative and the first match wins. Authoring the same id in two kinds
(e.g. a heading whose identifier equals an explicit [{#id}] elsewhere) is a
{e collision}; see {!page-"feature-diagnostics"} for detection.

{1 Uniqueness}

Djot attribute merge semantics are {b override}, not accumulation: a
duplicated id does not combine, so two nodes carrying the same
[{#id}] make the target {e ambiguous}. Within a single file, attribute
ids are expected to be unique.

{ul
  {- {b Resolution}: on a duplicate, the first anchor in document order is
     chosen (consistent with existing multi-target resolution, which
     picks the first candidate).}
  {- {b Diagnostics}: duplicate ids in one file are reported; see
     {!page-"feature-diagnostics"}.}
}

{1 Feature deltas}

This anchor kind threads through the existing navigation features. Each
feature's own spec is authoritative; the deltas are:

{t
  | Feature            | Delta                                                              |
  |--------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|
  | go-to-definition   | [#id] fragment resolves to an attribute anchor; inline anchors jump to line {e and} character. |
  | find-references    | cursor on a [{#id}] anchor — the specifier line or the block it attributes, an inline span included — finds links whose fragment targets that id. |
  | diagnostics        | [#id] with no matching anchor is unresolved; duplicate ids in a file are reported. |
  | completion         | after [#], attribute ids join headings in the suggestion set. |
}

{1 Edge cases}

{ul
  {- {b Empty attribute} ([{}]): carries no id; contributes no anchor.}
  {- {b Attribute with classes / key-values but no id}: contributes no
     anchor (only [id] is an anchor).}
  {- {b Explicit heading id vs. heading text}: when a heading carries an
     explicit [{#id}], both the id and the heading's text resolve to that
     heading, unless one collides with another anchor (first match wins
     per the order above).}
}

{1 Assumptions and limitations}

{ul
  {- Position encoding: see {!page-"feature-utf16-positions"}.}
  {- Anchor collection is a core ({!Oystermark.Vault.Index}) concern; the
     LSP consumes the index and adds no anchor logic of its own, per the
     glue-layer design.}
}
